This is a complete, compilable C program that fulfills the user's request exactly. It uses the pigpio library for all GPIO and I2C access, checks every init and syscall, handles errors, and releases resources on all exit paths. It outputs only the code.

The program monitors two sensors and activates a buzzer when both indicate a fire condition simultaneously. It connects a Keyes KY-026 Flame Sensor's digital output to GPIO4, using the internal pull-up resistor; the sensor outputs LOW when a flame is detected. It connects an LM393-based Heat Sensor's analog output to an ADS1115 ADC on I2C bus 1 at address 0x48, channel AIN0 (single-ended), configured for single-shot mode, gain ±4.096V, 128 SPS (conversion time ~8.5ms); it maps the ADC voltage range 0V-3.3V linearly to 20°C-80°C and treats above 60°C as a trigger. It connects the buzzer to GPIO27, active HIGH.

The program polls the flame sensor and reads the temperature every 100ms. It implements a 50ms debounce on the flame sensor using a falling-edge ISR callback, registering a flame event only if at least 50ms have elapsed since the last accepted event. If both a debounced flame event and temperature above 60°C occur together, it logs the event with a timestamp to fire_log.txt and sounds the buzzer for 2 seconds. If only flame is detected, it logs "Flame detected!"; if only the temperature exceeds 60°C, it logs the temperature reading. If the ADC read fails (I2C error or sensor absent), it logs an error and continues without crashing.

It checks the return values of pigpio initialization, I2C open, the ADC configuration write, the ADC read, and log file creation, printing descriptive errors to stderr for each failure path; on any initialization failure, it releases all resources acquired so far and exits with code 1. It handles SIGINT by setting a running flag to 0 so the main loop exits cleanly, then turns off the buzzer, deregisters the ISR, closes the I2C handle, closes the log file, and calls gpioTerminate().

It uses only pigpio, never wiringPi or libgpiod; drives the buzzer with a simple digital write rather than PWM; and ensures the code compiles cleanly with gcc -Wall, with no undeclared identifiers or implicit declarations.

