# How can I use Python and TensorFlow to create a neural network?
// plain

To use Python and TensorFlow to create a neural network, you need to install the TensorFlow library. You can do this by running the following command in your terminal:

```
pip install tensorflow
```

Once the library is installed, you can start creating your neural network. To do this, you need to import the TensorFlow library into your Python script. You can do this by adding the following line of code to your script:

```
import tensorflow as tf
```

Once the library is imported, you can start creating the layers of your neural network. To do this, you need to use the `tf.keras.layers` module. You can create a layer by using the following code:

```
layer = tf.keras.layers.Dense(units=100, activation='relu')
```

This code creates a layer with 100 units and a ReLU activation function.

Once you have created the layers of your neural network, you can start creating the model. To do this, you need to use the `tf.keras.Sequential` module. You can create a model by using the following code:

```
model = tf.keras.Sequential([layer])
```

This code creates a model with the layer that you created earlier.

Once you have created the model, you can start training it. To do this, you need to use the `model.compile` method. You can compile the model by using the following code:

```
model.compile(optimizer='adam', loss='sparse_categorical_crossentropy', metrics=['accuracy'])
```

This code compiles the model with the Adam optimizer, a sparse categorical crossentropy loss function, and an accuracy metric.

Once the model is compiled, you can start training it. To do this, you need to use the `model.fit` method. You can train the model by using the following code:

```
model.fit(x_train, y_train, epochs=10)
```

This code trains the model for 10 epochs.

Once the model is trained, you can start using it to make predictions. To do this, you need to use the `model.predict` method. You can make predictions by using the following code:

```
predictions = model.predict(x_test)
```

This code makes predictions on the test data.

Once you have made the predictions, you can start evaluating the model. To do this, you need to use the `model.evaluate` method. You can evaluate the model by using the following code:

```
model.evaluate(x_test, y_test)
```

This code evaluates the model on the test data.

Once you have evaluated the model, you can start using it to make predictions.

