### Explanation:
This program captures MJPEG video frames from a USB webcam connected to the Raspberry Pi 4 Model B. It uses the V4L2 API directly to configure the video capture, and the memory-mapped (MMAP) buffers for frame capture. It handles input and output asynchronously with select() so the program never blocks indefinitely waiting for a frame. It implements robust error handling for every V4L2 ioctl call, every file operation, and every memory mapping, and ensures clean resource release on all exit paths, including a graceful shutdown triggered by SIGINT.

The program starts by opening the video device file "/dev/video0" in read-write mode. It then queries the video device capabilities using the VIDIOC_QUERYCAP ioctl call.

Next, the program sets the video format using the VIDIOC_S_FMT ioctl call. It specifies the desired frame width, height, pixel format, and field type.

The program then requests video buffers using the VIDIOC_REQBUFS ioctl call. It specifies the buffer type, memory type, and the number of buffers to request.

The program allocates memory for the video buffers using malloc(). It then maps each video buffer to memory using the mmap() system call.

The program queues all video buffers for capture using the VIDIOC_QBUF ioctl call.

The program starts video capture using the VIDIOC_STREAMON ioctl call.

The program opens the frame log file in write mode.

The program enters a loop that captures frames and logs their timestamps. It uses the select() system call to wait for a video buffer to be available for reading. If a video buffer is available, the program dequeues it using the VIDIOC_DQBUF ioctl call. It then gets the frame timestamp using the VIDIOC_G_INPUT ioctl call. The program logs the frame timestamp to the frame log file.

The program then queues the video buffer for capture again using the VIDIOC_QBUF ioctl call.

The program handles errors by printing error messages and cleaning up resources. It ensures that all resources are released on all exit paths, including a graceful shutdown triggered by SIGINT.

This program demonstrates how to use the V4L2 API directly to configure and capture video frames from a USB webcam, and how to handle input and output asynchronously with select(). It also shows how to implement robust error handling and clean resource release on all exit paths.

