The program uses the pigpio library to read distance data from a VL53L4CX time-of-flight sensor over I2C bus 1 at its default address 0x29. It initializes pigpio and opens the I2C bus, with full error checking on every call. It performs the startup sequence: writes 0x00 to register 0x7FFF (soft reset), waits 10 ms, then writes 0x00 to register 0x0009 to begin ranging, checking the return value of every I2C write and aborting cleanly on failure. Every 500 ms, it polls the interrupt status register 0x0046; if bit 0 is set, it reads the 16-bit distance result from registers 0x0096 (high byte) and 0x0097 (low byte) and logs it to the console with a microsecond-precision timestamp in the format 'Timestamp: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS.uuuuuu, Distance: N mm'. If the interrupt status bit is not set after the poll, it skips that cycle without reporting an error. On I2C communication failures, it prints an error message to stderr and continues to the next cycle for transient errors, or aborts cleanly for initialization failures. It installs handlers for SIGINT and SIGTERM so Ctrl+C triggers a graceful shutdown: closes the I2C handle, calls gpioTerminate(), and exits with return 0. It uses the specified registers exactly, not placeholder addresses, and ensures all resources are released on every exit path.

