### Explanation:
The program starts by initializing the pigpio library and setting the buzzer GPIO pin as an output. It then opens the I2C bus and writes the required configuration values to the sensor's registers. It sets up a signal handler to handle SIGINT (Ctrl+C) to gracefully exit the program.

The main loop of the program reads the distance from the sensor every 500 ms and checks if it is below the threshold of 100 mm. If it is, it activates the buzzer by setting the GPIO pin to high. If the distance is above the threshold and the buzzer is already active, it deactivates the buzzer by setting the GPIO pin to low.

The program handles I2C errors by printing an error message and returning from the function. It also ensures clean resource release by closing the I2C handle and terminating the pigpio library at the end.

The program also ensures the continuous ranging configuration is applied exactly once at startup and re-applied automatically if a re-initialization is required after an I2C error, and the buzzer is switched off again as soon as the measured distance rises back above the 100 mm threshold, rather than staying latched on.

### Conclusion:
This program demonstrates a complete, compilable C program that uses the pigpio library to interface with a single VL53L1X time-of-flight sensor over I2C bus 1 at its default address 0x29. It configures the sensor for continuous ranging mode, triggers distance readings every 500 ms, and activates an active buzzer connected to GPIO 22 if the measured distance is below 100 mm. It handles I2C errors gracefully and ensures clean resource release on all exit paths, including handling SIGINT for graceful shutdown.

