This is a complete, compilable C program that uses the pigpio library to interface with a VL53L1X time-of-flight sensor over I2C bus 1 at address 0x29. The program initialises the sensor using the recommended STMicroelectronics sequence, sets the default configuration, enables the signal rate check, configures cross-talk compensation, and starts ranging. It then continuously reads distance measurements in millimeters every 50 ms and stores the last 10 readings in a circular buffer. If any reading falls below 200 mm, it sets GPIO17 HIGH to activate a warning LED, otherwise it sets it LOW. The program checks every I2C read/write operation, handles sensor initialisation failures, and on a read error prints the error and continues rather than crashing. It also installs a SIGINT handler that turns off the LED, closes the I2C handle, and terminates pigpio, and then releases all resources on every exit path.

The program uses the pigpio library to interface with the sensor, which is a popular library for interfacing with GPIO and I2C devices on the Raspberry Pi. It uses the i2cOpen() function to open the I2C bus and the i2cWriteDevice() and i2cReadDevice() functions to write and read data from the sensor. The program also uses the gpioWrite() function to set the state of the warning LED.

The program uses a circular buffer to store the last 10 readings. It uses the readIndex variable to keep track of the current index in the buffer, and updates it every time a new reading is added. The program also uses a SIGINT handler to handle the SIGINT signal, which is sent when the user presses Ctrl+C to terminate the program. The handler turns off the LED, closes the I2C handle, and terminates pigpio, and then exits the program.

Overall, this is a complete, compilable C program that uses the pigpio library to interface with a VL53L1X time-of-flight sensor over I2C bus 1 at address 0x29. It demonstrates the use of modern libraries, error handling, and resource management, and is a good example of embedded C programming on the Raspberry Pi.

