# Challenge 2: 3-Way Tracing Sensor with Interrupts

## Challenge Description

You are an embedded C programmer working on a 3-Way Tracing Sensor project. The sensor has three digital inputs that are connected to the Raspberry Pi's GPIOs. The sensor detects the position of an object by sensing the reflection of light from the object. The sensor has three LEDs that indicate the position of the object.

Your task is to write an embedded C program that reads the values of the three digital inputs from the sensor and prints them to the console every 50 ms. You also need to handle interrupts from the sensor to detect changes in the object's position.

## Challenge Solution

Here is a complete C program that reads the values of the 3-Way Tracing Sensor and prints them to the console every 50 ms. It uses the pigpio library to initialize the GPIOs and read their values. It also handles interrupts from the sensor to detect changes in the object's position.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pigpio.h>

#define LEFT_GPIO 17
#define CENTER_GPIO 27
#define RIGHT_GPIO 22

void handleInterrupt(int gpio, int level, uint32_t tick) {
    printf("Interrupt on GPIO %d, level %d, tick %d\n", gpio, level, tick);
}

int main() {
    if (gpioInitialise() < 0) {
        printf("Failed to initialize GPIOs\n");
        return 1;
    }

    gpioSetMode(LEFT_GPIO, PI_INPUT);
    gpioSetMode(CENTER_GPIO, PI_INPUT);
    gpioSetMode(RIGHT_GPIO, PI_INPUT);

    gpioSetAlertFunc(LEFT_GPIO, handleInterrupt);
    gpioSetAlertFunc(CENTER_GPIO, handleInterrupt);
    gpioSetAlertFunc(RIGHT_GPIO, handleInterrupt);

    while (1) {
        int left = gpioRead(LEFT_GPIO);
        int center = gpioRead(CENTER_GPIO);
        int right = gpioRead(RIGHT_GPIO);
        printf("Left: %d, Center: %d, Right: %d\n", left, center, right);
        gpioDelay(50000);
    }

    gpioTerminate();
    return 0;
}
```

This program demonstrates the use of pigpio library to read digital inputs from a 3-Way Tracing Sensor and printing their values to the console. It also handles interrupts from the sensor to detect changes in the object's position.

The program first initializes pigpio using gpioInitialise(). If the initialization fails, it prints an error message and exits with a non-zero status.

Next, it sets the modes of the three GPIOs using gpioSetMode().

The program then sets up interrupt handlers for the three GPIOs using gpioSetAlertFunc(). The handleInterrupt() function is called when an interrupt occurs on any of the GPIOs.

The main loop reads the values of the three GPIOs using gpioRead() and prints them to the console in the specified format. It uses gpioDelay(50000) to sleep for 50 ms between each iteration.

Finally, the program terminates pigpio using gpioTerminate() before exiting.

