### Explanation:
The program starts by initializing pigpio and opening the I2C bus. It then reads the PART_ID and HW_REV registers to verify the sensor is present and correct. If the sensor is absent or wrong, the program aborts.

The program then writes the LED current, averaging, and enable registers to configure the sensor. If any of these writes fail, the program aborts.

The program then opens the log file in append mode. If the log file cannot be opened, the program aborts.

The program enters a loop that reads the heart-rate register once per second. If the read fails, the program breaks out of the loop.

The program converts the raw heart-rate value to BPM and applies a 5-sample moving average. If the filtered BPM is between 30 and 220, the program appends the timestamp and BPM to the log file.

The program handles SIGINT gracefully, closing the I2C handle, terminating pigpio, and closing the log file on all exit paths.

The program uses robust error handling for sensor-absent and bus-error conditions throughout. It retries on transient I2C read errors without crashing.

The program uses modern libraries (pigpio) and checks every init and syscall, handling errors, and releasing resources on all exit paths.

