This program reads the gyroscope and accelerometer data from the ADIS16448 IMU over SPI using the Linux spidev ioctl interface and libgpiod for the chip-select/GPIO line. The program uses the single-register read protocol to read the gyroscope and accelerometer data from the appropriate registers. The gyroscope data is scaled to degrees per second (dps) and the accelerometer data is scaled to g. The program logs the data to the console every 100 ms.

The program opens the SPI device file using the open() function. It then sets the SPI mode, bits per word, and max speed using the ioctl() function. It opens the libgpiod chip using the gpiod_chip_open_by_number() function and gets the GPIO line using the gpiod_chip_get_line() function. It sets the line as an output using the gpiod_line_request_output() function.

The program sets a signal handler for SIGINT and SIGTERM using the signal() function. It then enters a main loop that reads the gyroscope and accelerometer data from the IMU and logs it to the console every 100 ms. It sets the chip-select line low using the gpiod_line_set_value() function. It then sends the register address with bit 7 set (0x80) in the first 16-bit SPI cycle using the ioctl() function. It reads the 16-bit data response in the second cycle using the ioctl() function. It scales the data to the appropriate units and logs it to the console. It sets the chip-select line high using the gpiod_line_set_value() function. It then sleeps for 100 ms using the usleep() function.

The program releases the GPIO line using the gpiod_line_release() function and closes the libgpiod chip using the gpiod_chip_close() function. It closes the SPI device file using the close() function.

The program handles errors by printing an error message and returning 1. It also handles SIGINT and SIGTERM signals by setting the keep_running variable to 0, which breaks out of the main loop and allows the program to cleanly release resources and exit.

This program demonstrates how to read data from an IMU over SPI using the Linux spidev ioctl interface and libgpiod for the chip-select/GPIO line. It also shows how to handle errors and signals in a robust way.

