The program opens the chip by name, not by number. This is a good practice because the chip number may change if the kernel renumbers the chip. The program requests the lines by number, not by name. This is a good practice because the line names may change if the kernel renames the lines.

The program requests the lines one at a time, not in bulk. This is a good practice because the bulk request is not atomic. If the program requests the lines in bulk, and the request fails, the program may have released the chip but not the lines.

