### Explanation:
The program opens the gpiochip by name, requests the PIR and relay GPIO lines, and logs failures to the system journal via syslog(3). It then enters a loop that polls the PIR line every 50 ms. If motion is detected (PIR reads HIGH), it energizes the relay (drive GPIO17 HIGH) for a 1-second strike duration, then de-energizes it (LOW). If the PIR sensor remains HIGH for more than 10 seconds continuously, it de-energizes the relay and logs a LOG_WARNING entry to the system journal describing a potential malfunction. It implements clean shutdown on SIGINT/SIGTERM: release all GPIO lines, close the chip, close the log, and exit, ensuring the relay is driven LOW before releasing the line.

### Notes:
- The program uses the modern libgpiod API (gpiod_line_request_input, gpiod_line_request_output, gpiod_line_get_value, gpiod_line_set_value), not wiringPi, to control the GPIO pins.
- The program checks every libgpiod call for errors and logs failures to the system journal via syslog(3).
- The program releases resources on all exit paths.
- The program outputs only the code.

