The program then defines the gpio_init() function, which opens the GPIO chip by name, gets the GPIO lines, and requests them. If any of these operations fail, the program exits with an error.

The program then defines the gpio_cleanup() function, which releases the GPIO lines and closes the GPIO chip.

The program then defines the gpio_set_value() function, which sets the value of a GPIO line. If the operation fails, the program exits with an error.

The program then defines the gpio_toggle_value() function, which toggles the value of a GPIO line. It gets the current value of the line, sets the opposite value, and if the operation fails, the program exits with an error.

The program then defines the gpio_toggle_values() function, which toggles the values of all GPIO lines. It calls the gpio_toggle_value() function for each line.

The program then defines the gpio_toggle_values_interval() function, which toggles the values of all GPIO lines for a specific interval. It uses clock_gettime() to get the start time, and then enters a loop that toggles the values, gets the current time, calculates the elapsed time, and checks if the elapsed time is greater than or equal to the specified interval. If so, it breaks out of the loop. Otherwise, it sleeps for 100 milliseconds.

The program then defines the gpio_toggle_values_intervals() function, which toggles the values of all GPIO lines for each interval. It calls the gpio_toggle_values_interval() function for each interval.

The program then defines the signal_handler() function, which cleans up the GPIO resources and exits the program.

The program then defines the main() function, which sets up the signal handlers for SIGINT and SIGTERM, initializes the GPIO resources, and enters an infinite loop that toggles the values of all GPIO lines for each interval.

The program then returns 0 to indicate successful execution.

