This is a complete, compilable C program that reads angular velocity from an MPU-6050 gyroscope on the hardware I2C bus (GPIO2/SDA, GPIO3/SCL) at address 0x68, using the Linux i2c-dev ioctl interface on /dev/i2c-1. The program uses the libgpiod library to access the I2C bus and read the sensor data.

The program first opens the I2C bus device file using the open() function. It then selects the I2C device using the ioctl() function with the I2C_SLAVE command.

The program then wakes up the sensor from sleep by writing to the PWR_MGMT_1 register (0x6B) with the value 0x00. It then sets the gyroscope full-scale range to ±250 dps by writing to the GYRO_CONFIG register (0x1B) with the value 0x00.

The program then sets up a signal handler for SIGINT and SIGTERM signals to cleanly exit the program when the user presses Ctrl+C or sends a SIGTERM signal.

The program then enters a loop that continuously reads the gyroscope data from the sensor. It reads 6 bytes of data starting from the GYRO_X register (0x43) using the read() function. The data is stored in a buffer called data.

The program then converts the raw signed 16-bit big-endian values to degrees/second by dividing each value by the gyroscope sensitivity factor of 131.0.

The program then logs the x, y, and z axis readings to the console using the printf() function.

The program then sleeps for 100 milliseconds using the usleep() function before reading the next set of gyroscope data.

The program closes the I2C bus device file and returns 0 to indicate successful execution.

