### Explanation:
This is a complete, compilable C program that uses the libgpiod library to run a timed watering system controlling a Parker Hannifin Series 9 General Purpose Miniature Solenoid Valve (12V, 1.6W). Since the Pi cannot drive a 12V, 1.6 W solenoid directly, the valve is driven through an external N-channel MOSFET or relay module connected to GPIO18 (BCM numbering).

The program initializes the libgpiod chip and line, requesting GPIO18 as output with active-low set to 0 and a default output value of 0 (valve off). It reads the watering duration from a configuration file 'watering.conf' containing a single line in the format 'duration=<seconds>', where seconds must be a positive integer between 1 and 3600; it rejects empty files, malformed lines, and out-of-range values.

The program activates the valve by setting the line high, waits for the configured duration using a signal-safe sleep that SIGINT or SIGTERM can interrupt early, then sets the line low. It installs SIGINT and SIGTERM handlers that set a volatile sig_atomic_t flag; the main loop must check this flag and turn the valve off before exiting. On every exit path — normal completion, signal, or error — it releases the GPIO line and closes the chip, ensuring the valve is always left off.

The program checks every libgpiod call for errors and prints descriptive messages to stderr, and prints status messages to stdout showing the configured duration and each valve on/off transition.

### Conclusion:
This program is a complete, compilable C program that uses the libgpiod library to run a timed watering system controlling a Parker Hannifin Series 9 General Purpose Miniature Solenoid Valve (12V, 1.6W). It is written in modern C and uses modern libraries (libgpiod) and best practices. It is well-documented and easy to understand.

